---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import PathwaySplit from '@/components/modelling/PathwaySplit.astro';
import DossierPanel from '@/components/modelling/DossierPanel.astro';
import ContrastPair from '@/components/modelling/ContrastPair.astro';
import ModellingVisual from '@/components/modelling/ModellingVisual.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="Make meaning a shared resource" description="A property model built with the people who understand the work. Discover the ideas, challenge the definitions, and explore the discipline behind a connected, context-first framework.">
  <p>
    Property information travels between people who ask different questions. A surveyor
    describes what was inspected. A conveyancer examines a title. A lender considers
    evidence for a decision. Connecting their data is useful only if we also understand
    what it describes, where its meaning holds and what it can support.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the work of semantic modelling. An ontology makes the important things,
    distinctions and relationships explicit. Our starting point is not a universal
    hierarchy imposed on every profession. It is the questions practitioners need to
    answer—and the agreements that let their meanings travel safely.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choose">Choose the question you want to answer</h2>
  <PathwaySplit primary={{
    href: '/semantic-modelling/understand', audience: 'Start here · no technical background needed',
    title: 'Why does shared meaning matter?',
    description: 'An introduction built around one recognisable property story. Learn what an ontology does, what it cannot do and how to read a model without learning its notation.',
    topics: ['A building is not its address or title record', 'One inspection can produce several report versions', 'Better questions are a valuable contribution'],
  }} alternatives={[
    { href: '/semantic-modelling/explore', audience: 'Explore the ideas', title: 'What are we actually modelling?', description: 'Things and identities, names and choices, contexts and connections. Follow the distinctions before inspecting their formal encoding.' },
    { href: '/semantic-modelling/contribute', audience: 'Bring your professional knowledge', title: 'How can I improve it?', description: 'Review a definition, supply an example, challenge an assumption or explain an exception. You do not need to write RDF.' },
    { href: '/semantic-modelling/method', audience: 'Modellers and implementers', title: 'What principles and rules do we follow?', description: 'The context-first manifesto and its technical contracts: eight modelling concerns, identity, mapping, validation, provenance and bounded standards profiles.' },
  ]} />

  <h2 id="one-story">One story, followed all the way through</h2>
  <DossierPanel name="Harbour Court" reference="HC · teaching case" stage="An inspection and a correction" facts={[
    { label: 'Place', value: '14 Orchard Road', note: 'A fictional address, not an identity rule.' },
    { label: 'Building', value: 'Two dwellings: Flat 1 and Flat 2' },
    { label: 'Inspection', value: '12 August 2026' },
    { label: 'Reports', value: 'Issued 14 August; corrected 20 August' },
  ]}>
    <p>Someone says, “The property has been inspected.” Which property? What was inspected? Which report describes it? Does the correction mean another visit took place?</p>
    <p>The same case runs through the section, from a plain-language explanation to the shape of a mapping record. You can see how a question becomes a distinction, an explicit relationship and something that can be reviewed.</p>
    <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/understand/a-property-story">Open the property story →</a></p>
  </DossierPanel>

  <h2 id="our-position">Local understanding. Explicit connections. Shared discipline.</h2>
  <ContrastPair leftLabel="Begin by fitting everything into one hierarchy" rightLabel="Begin with accountable meanings, then connect them">
    <Fragment slot="left"><p>A common hierarchy can be useful where its distinctions are already understood and agreed. Used as the starting answer to every problem, however, it can hide differences behind familiar names or force a local meaning into an unsuitable category.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="right"><p>Our approach starts with a bounded question, its evidence and the community responsible for the meaning. We make identity and relationships precise, reuse suitable standards and record the agreements needed at each boundary.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="verdict"><p>This is a choice about how to discover and govern meaning—not a claim that all ontology work is top-down, that every context invents its own truth, or that formal analysis is unnecessary.</p></Fragment>
  </ContrastPair>
  <p>
    The aim is a connected framework, not a collection of isolated glossaries.
    Foundational analysis challenges our distinctions. Shared vocabularies reduce
    duplication. Mappings explain correspondence without pretending different things
    are identical. A small common boundary earns its place through repeated, evidenced
    needs rather than growing into a compulsory master model.
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/method">Read the modelling manifesto</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="participation">The website is an interface to the model</h2>
  <p>
    You should not have to understand triples, namespaces or ontology tooling to say
    “that definition excludes a case we see every week”. Definitions, examples,
    relationships and questions are the public interface. Technical specialists turn
    reviewed meaning into precise artefacts; participants judge whether the meaning
    reflects the work.
  </p>
  <figure class="modelling-figure">
    <ModellingVisual kind="candidate-loop" />
    <figcaption>A useful modelling cycle makes disagreement inspectable. This is an explanatory handoff, not a claim that an automated approval workflow is deployed.</figcaption>
    <ol class="modelling-steps">
      <li><strong>Bring a question.</strong> Describe a situation, an ambiguity or an exchange that fails.</li>
      <li><strong>Make distinctions.</strong> Identify the subjects, definitions, relationships and missing evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Try examples and checks.</strong> Look for counterexamples as well as successful cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Record human judgement.</strong> Accept, revise or decline the proposal through the relevant governance process.</li>
      <li><strong>Revise and explain.</strong> Keep unresolved questions visible and make the next candidate reviewable.</li>
    </ol>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Start with <a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-a-definition">one definition</a>,
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/bring-evidence">one useful piece of evidence</a>,
    or <a href="/join">join a working group</a>. The participation guide distinguishes
    what you can do on the website today from the wider workflow being proposed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="credible-progress">Read the ambition and the evidence separately</h2>
  <p>
    The selected source-method ODRs govern the modelling discipline within OPDA's
    stated scope. The <a href="/development/property-pack">Property Pack</a> is a
    current machine-proposed component, not the whole framework or an adopted standard.
    A teaching example is neither of those. We identify these boundaries because a
    credible approach must be as clear about what is unfinished as about its ambition.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/standards-and-decisions">decision register</a>
    explains which rules apply and which proposals remain open. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/languages-and-profiles">standards profile</a>
    separates a language's meaning, the features we select and the implementation
    evidence actually available.
  </p>
</ModellingLayout>
